for more on what may come from the  summit we turn to Michael Mazar he's a  senior political scientist at the RAND  Corporation welcome to the table nice to  be here  so to Ben's report what are you  expecting from this meeting a meet and  greet or something substantial so  something in between I think the meet  and greet is the most important part  just to see how they get along it's too  short of a summit and there's not been  enough preparatory work done to expect  some kind of grand bargain where  everything is resolved but there should  be at least a road map that they can  agree to with maybe some initial steps  in the right direction you talk about a  road map if you look back over time  maybe go back to 1994 or even 2005 we've  had negotiations but more with North  Korea what do we learn from that that  makes a certain and what needs to be  done now well a couple of things one is  that North Korea has an incentive to  draw these things out and and delay and  find ways to kind of stretch the  negotiations without actually giving up  what it's trying to achieve but the  biggest question right now is how  different Kim jong-un actually is there  are tantalizing signals that he really  cares more about the economic prospects  of his country than his father or  grandfather what he's willing to give up  to get that right is the situation is  that well not at once not at once the  question is and the president Trump's  referred to a process here so the  question is can you get some initial  concessions that give everybody a sense  that North Korea's moving in the right  direction and a clear plan for over a  number of years I think everybody who  studies this realizes it will take a  while but at least if we have some clear  milestones that were moving toward we'll  know we're moving in the right direction  and that ought to be enough out of an  initial summit Michael when the  president says that he doesn't need much  preparation for a meeting like this or  that he's been preparing for it his  entire life does that give him the upper  hand does that put him at a disadvantage  or perhaps neutral  I don't know that really makes a lot of  difference I think clearly Secretary of  Pompeo has been working with the North  Koreans very closely it sounds like they  have some at least initial statements  agreed and the North Koreans have given  some assurance of a handful of things  they're willing to agree to but one that  we know from summits is by far the most  important thing is the personal  chemistry between the leaders that comes  up whether they develop trust where  they actually like each other I don't  know if that's possible in this case but  that's the most important thing I'm  going to be looking for is whether the  two leaders seem to be getting along and  establishing a basis of trust that can  move forward we've heard some talk in  the last week about a rift between  Pompeo and Bolton how does that factor  in when those are two of the closest  advisors to the president right now on  North Korea right yeah I mean I don't  know to what extent that's true I think  the way it factors in is it just gives  the president a choice always in those  cases it comes down to the president's  decision and the president will side  with one of the other side of his or  potentially her administration and then  that'll that'll be it unless one of the  other of those people then tries to work  under the table to subvert negotiations  or what the other side is doing I don't  get the sense of President Trump would  tolerate that from anybody so I think  he's made his choice which is to try to  move forward you know we have an open  and democratic society here so we'll  know how successful or unsuccessful this  meeting is how do you think it will play  in North Korea not just with the  hardliners in Kim's regime but the  people will they ever even get a sense  of what is happening in Singapore so  it'll play in two ways one is the way  the regime there wants it to play which  is of course the dominant way they will  broadcast whatever message they want to  and the primary message will be the  United States we forced the United  States to come to the table and they  recognized us in ways they never have  before that'll be their primary message  but there is a lot of information  getting into North Korea these days and  there is a sense that the North Korean  people have economic demands that they  haven't had in the past so there'll be a  short term message but then the  longer-term development of this whether  the North Korean people begin to see  benefits out of it relief of sanctions  that will be the longer-term more  important way in which a message gets  through to the North Korean people all  right all eyes on Singapore Tuesday  Michel Mazar thank you very much as  always CBS News will bring you coverage  of the historic meeting in Singapore  starting with our Sunday broadcast we  will also have updates throughout the  summit Jeff Glor will anchor the CBS  Evening News from Singapore on Monday  and Tuesday
 

